<div class="exercise"><div class="ex-q"><p>Why can’t you always reduce a diagram by series/parallel/feedback rules alone, without moving blocks?</p>
</div><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><div class="ex-s"><p>When loops overlap without nesting (a pick-off inside one loop feeds a summer inside another), no subsystem is a pure series, parallel or feedback connection. You have to move a junction or pick-off past a block first, or use Mason’s rule, which handles any topology.</p>
</div></details></div>
